tr=''.join(f'<tr><th scope="row">{name}</th><td>{w:g} × {h:g}</td><td>{w/25.4:.3f} × {h/25.4:.3f}</td><td>{math.ceil(round(w/25.4*300,10))} × {math.ceil(round(h/25.4*300,10))}</td></tr>' for name,w,h in rows)
calculator=page('photo-size-calculator','Photo size calculator: inches, mm and print pixels','Convert photo dimensions between inches, centimetres and millimetres and calculate pixel targets. Includes a common-size chart and print-quality examples.',f'''
<p class="answer">One inch is exactly <strong>25.4 mm</strong>. Convert millimetres to inches by dividing by 25.4. To calculate a print-resolution target, multiply each dimension in inches by your chosen PPI.</p>
<section class="calculator card" aria-labelledby="calc-heading"><h2 id="calc-heading">Calculate your photo size</h2><form id="size-form"><div class="calc-fields"><label>Width<input id="size-width" type="number" min="0.01" max="10000" step="any" value="2" required inputmode="decimal"></label><label>Height<input id="size-height" type="number" min="0.01" max="10000" step="any" value="2" required inputmode="decimal"></label><label>Units<select id="size-unit"><option value="in">Inches</option><option value="mm">Millimetres</option><option value="cm">Centimetres</option></select></label><label>Target PPI<select id="size-ppi"><option value="300">300 PPI</option><option value="200">200 PPI</option><option value="150">150 PPI</option></select></label></div><button type="submit">Calculate</button></form><output id="size-result" for="size-width size-height size-unit size-ppi" aria-live="polite"><strong>2 × 2 in = 50.8 × 50.8 mm = 5.08 × 5.08 cm</strong><br>At 300 PPI: at least 600 × 600 visible pixels.</output><p class="fine">Calculated in your browser. No photo upload. Pixel targets describe resolution after cropping, not identity-document compliance.</p><noscript><p>The calculator needs JavaScript. The conversion formulas and size chart below work without it.</p></noscript></section>
<h2>Common photo sizes</h2><div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>Dimensions and 300 PPI targets; decimal inches shown rounded to three places</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Size</th><th scope="col">Millimetres</th><th scope="col">Inches</th><th scope="col">Pixels at 300 PPI</th></tr></thead><tbody>{tr}</tbody></table></div>
<h2>Use the original units for exact printing</h2><p>The table rounds decimal inches for readability. For a 35 × 45 mm print, enter 35 and 45 in millimetres in the app instead of entering rounded inch values. For a 2 × 2 inch photo, enter inches: 50 × 50 mm is a smaller rectangle.</p>
<h2>How to calculate print pixels</h2><p>For a 4 × 6 inch print at 300 PPI, multiply 4 × 300 and 6 × 300: 1200 × 1800 pixels. For 35 × 45 mm, divide by 25.4 first and multiply by 300: approximately 413.39 × 531.50, rounded up to 414 × 532 pixels.</p>
<h2>Pixel dimensions do not decide the printed size</h2><p>The same file can be printed at several physical sizes. Its PPI changes with the chosen dimensions and crop. Photo Print lets you set physical dimensions directly and check quality in the finished layout. See <a href="photo-print-resolution-ppi.html">pixels, PPI and cropping</a>.</p>'''+related,kind='WebPage',script='<script src="assets/size-calculator.js" defer></script>')
